  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the Great Lakes will maintain\ntranquil weather through Friday. Light winds overnight may lead\nto a brief period of patchy ground fog early Friday morning,\nprimarily in interior northern Illinois. Otherwise, afternoon\nconditions on Friday remain comfortable with partly cloudy \nskies and high temperatures in the lower 80s.\n\nA shifting upper-level pattern will bring building heat this\nweekend as a Rockies upper-level ridge expands eastward. Strong\nwarm air advection and steepening lapse rates along its eastern\nperiphery may trigger scattered showers and thunderstorms late\nSaturday into Saturday night. While the primary focus remains \nacross southern Wisconsin, a 20-30% chance for precipitation \nextends into northern Illinois, mainly north of I-80. By Sunday,\nexpect a return to more humid conditions with highs reaching \nthe upper 80s to near 90.\n\nThe warm and humid airmass persists through next week. Forecast\nconfidence in daily highs remains moderate, as peak temperatures\nwill depend on convective activity along the northern periphery\nof a southern states ridge. Periodic waves of showers and\nthunderstorms are possible near northern Illinois. Should\nactivity remain displaced north into Wisconsin, afternoon\ntemperatures could top out into the low 90s.\n\nKJB",
